relations:\tabular{lll}{
   Operator \tab Relation \tab \code{e1 \%op\% e2} holds iff \cr
   \verb{\%p\%} \tab precedes \tab \code{e1} ends before \code{e2} begins (a gap) \cr
   \verb{\%m\%} \tab meets \tab \code{e1} ends exactly where \code{e2} begins (no gap, no overlap) \cr
   \code{\%o\%} \tab overlaps \tab \code{e1} begins before \code{e2}, \code{e1} ends between the start and end of \code{e2} \cr
   \verb{\%s\%} \tab starts \tab \code{e1} and \code{e2} begin together, and \code{e1} ends first \cr
   \verb{\%d\%} \tab during \tab \code{e1} is strictly within \code{e2}'s span \cr
   \verb{\%f\%} \tab finishes \tab \code{e1} and \code{e2} end together, and \code{e1} begins later \cr
   \code{==} \tab equals \tab \code{e1} and \code{e2} share both endpoints \cr
   \verb{\%pi\%} \tab preceded by \tab \code{e2 \%p\% e1} \cr
   \verb{\%mi\%} \tab met by \tab \code{e2 \%m\% e1} \cr
   \verb{\%oi\%} \tab overlapped by \tab \code{e2 \%o\% e1} \cr
   \verb{\%si\%} \tab started by \tab \code{e2 \%s\% e1} \cr
   \verb{\%di\%} \tab contains \tab \code{e2 \%d\% e1} \cr
   \verb{\%fi\%} \tab finished by \tab \code{e2 \%f\% e1} \cr
}


\link{mt_linear-compare}/\link{mt_cyclical-compare}'s \code{<}/\code{>} are a deliberate
deviations from their use as 'precedes' in Allen's interval algebra
(\verb{\%p\%}/\verb{\%pi\%} operators). The important difference is that \code{<}/\code{>} include
'meets' relations, while \verb{\%p\%}/\verb{\%pi\%} requires gaps, so in the examples
below \code{jan < feb} is TRUE in mixtime but the equivalent symbol in Allen's
interval algebra (\code{jan \%p\% feb}) is FALSE because there is no gap.

The implementation here also extends to continuous time instants, which are
treated as degenerate zero-width intervals. This allows testing how a
specific time instant relates to time spans, for example does January 2020
contain 30\% through the day 2020-01-24 is
\code{yearmonth("2020-01") \%di\% date(datetime("2020-01-24 07:12:00"), discrete = FALSE)}.
This is a technical deviation from Allen's interval algebra, which assumes
that intervals are non-degenerate (i.e. end > start, not end >= start).
}
\examples{
jan <- yearmonth("2020 Jan")
feb <- yearmonth("2020 Feb")
mar <- yearmonth("2020 Mar")
q1 <- yearquarter("2020 Q1")

jan == jan    # January equals itself

jan \%p\% mar   # January precedes March: February leaves a genuine gap
mar \%pi\% jan  # ... equivalently, March is preceded by January

jan \%m\% feb   # January meets February: adjacent, no gap
feb \%mi\% jan  # ... equivalently, February is met by January
jan < feb     # `<` also holds for adjacent pairs, unlike `\%p\%`
jan \%p\% feb   # FALSE: no gap between them, so they don't (strictly) precede

jan \%s\% q1    # January and Q1 start together, but January finishes first
q1 \%si\% jan   # ... equivalently, Q1 is started by January

feb \%d\% q1    # February is strictly within Q1
q1 \%di\% feb   # ... equivalently, Q1 contains February

mar \%f\% q1    # March and Q1 finish together, but March starts later
q1 \%fi\% mar   # ... equivalently, Q1 is finished by March

}
